// `ptr<U>(addr)` reinterprets an address as pointing at a different type. it takes an address,
// so its argument is almost always a `:$` expression. no alignment or size checking is done.
//
// it needs **no** `unsafe` block, and that placement is the design rather than an oversight: a
// `ptr<T>` promises nothing, so computing another raw address out of one - and writing through it -
// asserts nothing either. the word is owed one step later, where a raw address becomes a `T&`.
// the two halves are written out below, in that order

$n = 0;
ptr<int> $ints = &$n;

// the same memory, viewed as bytes. no word: still a raw address, and access through one is treated
// as if it could touch any memory at all
ptr<uint8> $bytes = ptr<uint8>($ints:$);
$bytes:$[0] = 255;

echo $n;

// a borrow widens to a nullable pointer implicitly - it only discards the non-null guarantee
$a = 5;
int& $r = &$a;
ptr<int> $p = $r:$;
$p = 8;
echo $a;

// the narrowing back is the **promotion**: it turns a raw address into a trusted typed borrow, and
// from there every access through `$back` is optimized as an `int32`. that is the assertion `unsafe`
// marks - not the reinterpretation above, which only computed another raw address
unsafe {
    int& $back = int&($p:$);
    $back = 12;
}
echo $a;
